(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physical-diagnosis-1-exam-2-hent-exam-quiz"]={"title":"HENT (Head, Ears, Nose, Throat) Examination — Set 1 (Physical Diagnosis 1)","category":"Physical Diagnosis","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"When inspecting the head, which features should be specifically noted?","choices":["Only whether or not the patient currently reports any headache symptoms","Only the patient's measured visual acuity result in each eye","Only the patient's current blood pressure and pulse rate readings","Size, shape, and contour of the skull, noting any deformity or lumps"],"answer":3,"exp":"Head inspection specifically includes assessing size, shape, and contour of the skull for deformity or lumps. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"During face inspection, which findings should the examiner assess?","choices":["Only the size of the patient's pupils, assessed bilaterally","Asymmetry, expression, edema, scars, and involuntary movements","Only the turgor of the patient's overlying facial skin tissue","Only the strength of the patient's jaw against resistance"],"answer":1,"exp":"These are the specific face inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"During palpation of the head, what should the examiner assess?","choices":["Only the range of motion of the patient's cervical neck","Only auscultation for bruits overlying the scalp and skull","Only the patient's peripheral nail-bed capillary refill time","Areas of tenderness, deformity, masses, and hair texture"],"answer":3,"exp":"These are the specific findings assessed on head palpation per the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 13"},{"q":"Which structure of the tympanic membrane is the small, loose portion located above the malleus?","choices":["Pars tensa","Pars flaccida","Umbo","Cone of light"],"answer":1,"exp":"The pars flaccida is the small, loose superior portion of the tympanic membrane. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 17"},{"q":"When inspecting the tympanic membrane with the otoscope, which features should be assessed?","choices":["Only the presence of visible cerumen within the ear canal","Only tenderness elicited over the mastoid process itself","Color, contour, injection, perforation, opacities, and distortions","Only the symmetry of the two external auricles bilaterally"],"answer":2,"exp":"These are the specific TM (tympanic membrane) inspection findings listed, along with landmarks such as the malleus, umbo, pars tensa/flaccida, and cone of light. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 20"},{"q":"When using the otoscope to examine the ear, how should the auricle be manipulated to straighten the canal?","choices":["Push the auricle flat and firmly against the side of the scalp","Pull the auricle up, back, and slightly away from the head","Pull the auricle downward and forward, toward the patient's face","No manipulation of the auricle is needed at any point"],"answer":1,"exp":"This maneuver straightens the ear canal, which is naturally curved, for better otoscopic visualization. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 22"},{"q":"When inserting the otoscope into the ear canal, what technique should the examiner use?","choices":["Insert the speculum quickly and forcefully to minimize the patient's overall discomfort","Hold the otoscope loosely without bracing the hand against the patient's head","Angle the otoscope tip upward and backward, away from the ear canal floor","Brace the hand against the patient's head, insert gently, and angle down and forward"],"answer":3,"exp":"Bracing the hand against the head prevents injury from sudden patient movement, and the otoscope should be inserted gently, angled down and forward. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 23"},{"q":"During the whispered voice test, how should the examiner position and prepare the patient?","choices":["Stand 2 feet behind the patient, occlude the non-tested ear, and rub the tragus","Have the patient close their eyes but leave both ears completely unoccluded","Stand directly in front of the patient at approximately an arm's length","Perform the test only in a completely silent room, using no masking maneuver"],"answer":0,"exp":"The examiner stands 2 feet behind the patient, occludes the ear not being tested, and rubs the tragus to mask sound to that ear. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"During the whispered voice test, what constitutes a normal result?","choices":["There is no defined scoring threshold for this test","The patient correctly repeats 1 out of 6 whispered letters/numbers","The patient correctly repeats 3 out of 6 whispered letters/numbers","The patient correctly repeats 6 out of 6 whispered letters/numbers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Normal is defined as 3 out of 6 items correctly repeated (note: the lecture flags 4/6 as an abnormal result to highlight care in scoring). — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"If an abnormality is found on hearing screening, which further tests help distinguish conductive from sensorineural hearing loss?","choices":["Transillumination and palpation","Weber and Rinne tuning fork tests","Whispered voice test repeated a second time","Otoscopic examination alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"The Weber and Rinne tests are used to further evaluate and distinguish conductive versus sensorineural hearing loss. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 25"},{"q":"On nasal inspection, which findings should be assessed?","choices":["Only the distribution and density of visible nasal hair within each nostril, with no other structural findings recorded","Only the presence or absence of a visibly deviated nasal septum, with no other mucosal findings recorded at all","Deformities, asymmetry, polyps, nares/turbinates, mucosa color/swelling/bleeding, exudate character, and septal deviation/inflammation/perforation","Only tenderness elicited on direct palpation over the frontal and maxillary sinuses, with no other findings assessed"],"answer":2,"exp":"These are the comprehensive nasal inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 34"},{"q":"When using the otoscope to examine the nose, what technique should be used?","choices":["Use the smallest available speculum, tilt the patient's head forward, and press directly on the septum","Hold the otoscope handle straight up and insert the tip directly against the septum itself","Use the largest speculum, tilt the patient's head back, hold the handle to the side, and avoid the septum","No speculum attachment is needed at all for a proper nasal examination, since the naked eye is sufficient"],"answer":2,"exp":"This is the correct technique for otoscopic nasal examination as described in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 35"},{"q":"When palpating the sinuses, how should the frontal sinuses be assessed?","choices":["No palpation of sinuses is performed; only transillumination is used","Press up from under the brow ridge, avoiding the eyes","Press down on the bridge of the nose","Press directly on the eyes for tenderness"],"answer":1,"exp":"Frontal sinus palpation involves pressing up from under the brow ridge while avoiding the eyes. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 36"},{"q":"During sinus transillumination, what technique and finding are used to assess sinus air-fluid status?","choices":["Transillumination is performed using the otoscope's light source placed directly into the external ear canal, rather than on the face at all","Keep the exam room brightly lit throughout the entire test and shine the light source directly into the nares only, never onto the face or forehead at all","Darken the room, place the light source on the maxilla or under the brow ridge, and look for a glow on the hard palate or forehead; decreased/absent glow suggests the sinus is not filled with air","A strong, bright glow observed on the hard palate or forehead during this particular test always indicates that active bacterial sinusitis is definitely present"],"answer":2,"exp":"This is the correct transillumination technique and interpretation described in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 37"},{"q":"Before beginning the oral cavity exam, what should the examiner ask the patient to do?","choices":["Remove dentures or dental appliances","Rinse with mouthwash immediately before the exam","Apply topical anesthetic to the oral mucosa","Refrain from speaking throughout the exam"],"answer":0,"exp":"Dentures/dental appliances should be removed before inspection to allow full visualization of the oral mucosa and gums. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"When inspecting the buccal mucosa, which duct opening should specifically be identified?","choices":["Wharton's duct (submandibular)","Stensen's duct (parotid)","The thoracic duct","The nasolacrimal duct"],"answer":1,"exp":"Stensen's duct, the opening of the parotid gland, is specifically identified during buccal mucosa inspection. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 49"},{"q":"When inspecting the floor of the mouth, which duct opening should be identified?","choices":["The lacrimal punctum opening","Wharton's duct (submandibular)","Stensen's duct (parotid)","The eustachian tube opening"],"answer":1,"exp":"Wharton's duct, the submandibular gland's duct opening, is identified during floor-of-mouth inspection. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"When assessing the soft palate and uvula, what specific maneuver is used?","choices":["Shine a light source directly into the external ear canal","Ask the patient to say \"ah\" and observe for symmetrical rise/fall","Ask the patient to hold their breath for a full ten seconds","Palpate the uvula directly with a single gloved index finger"],"answer":1,"exp":"Having the patient say \"ah\" allows observation of symmetric elevation of the soft palate/uvula, which also screens vagus (CN (cranial nerve) X) function. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 50"},{"q":"When inspecting the tonsillar pillars and tonsils, which findings should be assessed?","choices":["Only the presence of a gag reflex","Redness, swelling, exudate, ulceration, and hypertrophy","Only tonsil size graded on a 0-4 scale with no other findings","Only symmetry of the uvula"],"answer":1,"exp":"These are the specific tonsillar inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 51"},{"q":"When using a tongue blade to visualize the pharynx, what technique should be used?","choices":["Avoid touching the tongue entirely and rely on the patient opening wide","Press firmly on the midpoint of the tongue, without going too far back","Insert the tongue blade as far posteriorly as possible","Press lightly only on the tip of the tongue"],"answer":1,"exp":"Firm pressure at the tongue midpoint provides visualization while avoiding the far posterior tongue, which can trigger a strong gag reflex. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 51"},{"q":"When inspecting the pharyngeal wall, which findings should be assessed?","choices":["Post-nasal drip, exudate, and erythema","Only the color of the hard palate","Only the size of the visible tonsils","Only the quality of the patient's voice"],"answer":0,"exp":"These are the specific pharyngeal wall inspection findings listed in the lecture. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 51"},{"q":"On neck inspection, which findings should be assessed?","choices":["Only the presence or absence of noticeable hoarseness in the patient's speaking voice","Only the patient's overall active and passive cervical range of motion","Only the quality and amplitude of the bilateral carotid pulses on palpation","Symmetry, masses, scars, gland enlargement, visible lymph nodes, and tracheal position"],"answer":3,"exp":"These are the comprehensive neck inspection findings listed, including trachea midline versus deviation and thyroid symmetry/masses/rise with swallowing. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"When palpating the trachea for position, what technique is used?","choices":["No physical bedside technique exists for this at all; tracheal position can only be reliably assessed using chest imaging studies","Stand directly behind the seated patient and use both thumbs simultaneously to press firmly on either side of the trachea near the sternal notch","Stand in front of the patient, place an index finger on each side of the trachea, and evaluate the distance to the sternocleidomastoid in several locations, which should be symmetrical","Ask the patient to swallow repeatedly while the examiner auscultates directly over the tracheal air column with the stethoscope bell"],"answer":2,"exp":"This is the correct technique described for assessing tracheal position/midline status. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 61"},{"q":"When inspecting the thyroid as part of neck inspection, what maneuver helps assess it?","choices":["Ask the patient to turn their head fully to one side only, then observe the gland","Ask the patient to hold their breath and observe for jugular venous distension","Observe for symmetry, masses, and rise/fall of the gland while the patient swallows","No functional maneuver is needed here; only static visual inspection is performed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Asking the patient to swallow while observing the thyroid helps assess symmetry, masses, and normal gland movement. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 60"},{"q":"During anterior thyroid palpation, what is the recommended technique?","choices":["Stand behind the seated patient and use only the left hand to palpate the entire thyroid gland","Use a wooden tongue blade to gently press on the visible thyroid gland from outside the neck","Stand facing the patient and palpate the thyroid gland outline and isthmus to confirm inspection findings","Palpate the gland only with the patient's neck held fully extended backward the entire time"],"answer":2,"exp":"The anterior approach involves facing the patient and palpating the outline/isthmus to confirm what was seen on inspection. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 62"},{"q":"In the posterior thyroid palpation technique, how is the right lobe palpated?","choices":["Palpate the right lobe with the right hand while displacing the trachea to the right using the left hand","Palpate the right lobe with the left hand while displacing the trachea to the left instead","Palpate both lobes simultaneously with both hands, without displacing the trachea at all","Stand in front of the patient directly, rather than behind, for this specific maneuver"],"answer":0,"exp":"This is the correct posterior technique: right hand palpates the right lobe while the left hand displaces the trachea toward that side. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"During the posterior thyroid exam, what maneuver helps the examiner feel the isthmus?","choices":["Ask the patient to hold their breath while pressing above the thyroid cartilage","Place fingers below the cricoid cartilage and ask the patient to swallow","Have the patient hyperextend the neck while the examiner auscultates","No specific maneuver is needed; the isthmus is always easily palpable at rest"],"answer":1,"exp":"Placing fingers below the cricoid cartilage while the patient swallows allows the examiner to feel the isthmus move under the fingers. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 63"},{"q":"How many groups of cervical lymph nodes are described in the lecture?","choices":["5 groups","15 groups","10 groups","3 groups"],"answer":2,"exp":"The lecture lists 10 cervical lymph node groups, including occipital, pre-auricular, posterior auricular, tonsillar, submandibular, submental, superficial cervical, posterior cervical, deep cervical, and supraclavicular. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 64"},{"q":"What technique should be used to palpate cervical lymph nodes?","choices":["Use the whole flat palm pressed firmly against the side of the neck","Use the thumb pressed firmly along a single straight line down the neck","Percuss the neck directly using a standard neurologic reflex hammer","Use the pads of the index and middle fingers in gentle circular motions"],"answer":3,"exp":"Cervical node palpation uses the finger pads (index and middle) in gentle circles. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 65"},{"q":"When a lymph node is palpated, what maneuver helps differentiate it from a muscle or artery?","choices":["Apply ice to the area and observe the response","Auscultate over the node with a stethoscope","Ask the patient to cough forcefully","Roll the node up-down and side-to-side"],"answer":3,"exp":"Rolling the node in multiple directions (up-down, side-to-side) under the fingers helps distinguish a discrete lymph node from a muscle band or pulsatile artery. — Physical Examination Head, Ears, Nose, HENT (head, ears, nose, throat) lecture, Slide 66"}]};
